I can help — start by confirming a few missing inputs, then I’ll calculate CAC and LTV and draft a short finance narrative for you. Quick notes first:

1) I see a possible inconsistency: you wrote $5.2M cash, 14 months runway, burn rate $62K/month. 5.2M / 62K ≈ 84 months, so either the stated burn is net of revenue or the runway number is using a different burn figure. I’ll flag that for you to reconcile.

What I need from you (or I can draft a data-request email to get these from Finance/Marketing):
- Marketing + sales spend for the period you want to measure (quarterly or annual; include agency fees, ad spend, events, sales commissions, S&M headcount).
- Number of new customers acquired in the same period (or cost per new account by channel).
- Total number of active customers (for ARPU) or ARPU directly (average revenue per customer per month/quarter).
- Gross margin (or contribution margin) % on revenue.
- Churn rate (monthly or quarterly) or average customer lifetime in months.
- Time horizon for CAC payback you care about (monthly, quarters, 12 months).

Formulas and how I'll compute them
- CAC = (Total Sales + Marketing spend in period) / (Number of new customers acquired in that same period).
- Average Revenue per User (ARPU) = (Revenue in period) / (Average number of customers in period). Use monthly or quarterly units consistently.
- Average customer lifetime (in months) ≈ 1 / (monthly churn). If churn is quarterly, use 1 / quarterly churn in quarters.
- LTV (simplified) = ARPU (per month) × Gross margin % × Average lifetime (in months).
  - Alternative cohort / discounted approach: sum discounted future gross profits — use if you want precision.
- LTV:CAC ratio target: ideally ~3:1. Payback period target: <12 months for many SaaS/recurring businesses (benchmarks vary by industry).

Illustrative example (so you can see the math)
- Use your revenue last quarter = $1.8M.
- Suppose:
  - New customers last quarter = 1,000
  - S&M spend last quarter = $300,000
  - Total customers = 9,000 (so quarterly ARPU = 1.8M / 9,000 = $200 per quarter → ≈ $66.67/month)
  - Gross margin = 60%
  - Monthly churn = 3% → avg lifetime ≈ 33.3 months
- Then:
  - CAC = $300,000 / 1,000 = $300
  - Monthly ARPU = $66.67
  - LTV = 66.67 × 0.60 × 33.3 ≈ $1,333
  - LTV:CAC ≈ 1,333 / 300 ≈ 4.4 → healthy by common benchmarks
  - Payback months = CAC / (monthly ARPU × gross margin) = 300 / (66.67 × 0.6) ≈ 7.5 months

Suggested finance narrative bullets (short, deployable in a memo or presentation)
- Current high-level position: cash runway, revenue trend, and our S&M efficiency (CAC, LTV).
- Methodology: what period, definition of “customer”, gross margin assumptions, churn measurement.
- Key metrics and benchmarks: CAC, ARPU, LTV, LTV:CAC ratio, payback period — show trend vs prior periods.
- Actionable conclusions:
  - If LTV:CAC < 3: investigate pricing, upsell, retention, or reduce acquisition cost by channel.
  - If payback > 12 months: consider reallocating spend to lower-cost channels or tighten onboarding to reduce churn.
  - If we suspect runway miscalculation, reconcile net burn vs gross burn (include revenue offsets).
- Next steps: get channel-level CAC, cohort LTVs, and run a sensitivity analysis.

How I can help next (pick one)
- I can compute CAC and LTV now if you provide the numbers I asked for.
- I can draft a two-paragraph internal email to Finance & Marketing requesting the missing inputs (I’ll include the exact fields to collect).
- I can draft a short internal memo summarizing the results and recommended actions once we have the numbers.

